    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "90f832bb_6cd80d1d",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1061164
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-20T15:30:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the real question here is why someone with min_sdk_version set depends on these targets? I don\u0027t know the historic context here, but that seems like a tech debt from the past?",
      "parentUuid": "6bbe5007_c4cd1f4c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "52ab56b583cc86a6771eb8fa6d8afa21b6fbb08b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "217a31a2_0981a270",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-20T15:46:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see a few: https://cs.corp.google.com/search/?q\u003df:cts+c:min_sdk_version+f:%5C.bp+-c:%5C+sdk_version\u0026m\u003d100\u0026sort\u003d1\u0026sq\u003dpackage:%5Eandroid$\u0026type\u003dcs\n\nThey specify min_sdk_version but not sdk_version, which means that are by default linked to \u0027framework\u0027. By definition, all cts tests should built against SDK, but certain tests actually depend on private APIs for various reasons.\n\nYeah, these are technical debts, but even so, I don\u0027t think they can justify that we have to set min_sdk_version also for framework, ext, etc.\n\nI think we don\u0027t need to check this across dynamic dependencies (i.e. libs), but only for static dependencies, because that\u0027s where the dependency becomes part of the dependent.",
      "parentUuid": "90f832bb_6cd80d1d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "52ab56b583cc86a6771eb8fa6d8afa21b6fbb08b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aec1bfe2_ce9bae46",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1061164
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-21T15:00:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Totally agree, I don\u0027t expect we mark framework with min_sdk_version. That just doesn\u0027t make sense. This list to \"burndown\" violations, which could mean adding minsdkversion props, or fixing deps, or making the exceptions part of the full workflow. I didn\u0027t fully understand each of these exceptions, so I just put them all into a single burndown list.\n\nThe last bit is something I am not 100% positive about though. It doesn\u0027t really matter whether the dependency is static or not, as long as you are expecting it to function on certain platforms. If its a libs dependency, then you are likely expecting it to be available at runtime to functioning according to specification. Why would that not classify for min_sdk_version?\n\n(There are nuances, where in java world as long as you guarantee that the code path is not executed, it\u0027s okay. So we don\u0027t need a full check for minSdkVersion, but that will be fixed after we introduce lint).",
      "parentUuid": "217a31a2_0981a270",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "52ab56b583cc86a6771eb8fa6d8afa21b6fbb08b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f5d37e54_9f038031",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-22T04:29:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For the last point:\n\njava_library {name: \"libA\", libs: [\"libB\"]} doesn\u0027t mean that libB.jar will be used at runtime. It could be a entirely different library in the classpath which will serve the APIs that libB provided to libA at build-time. (Consider android.jar as libB and framework.jar as libC) So, whether libB.jar supports a certain SDK or not doesn\u0027t affect libA.jar\u0027s ability to run on the SDK.",
      "parentUuid": "aec1bfe2_ce9bae46",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "52ab56b583cc86a6771eb8fa6d8afa21b6fbb08b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c39940ac_0f63abf5",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1061164
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-22T09:16:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t fully grasp realities of platform side, but purely from java/build perspective: If you are compiling against libB APIs, then you are potentially using them at runtime. How do we guarantee that you aren\u0027t 100% using them at runtime on the wrong platform? Ideally, at some point lint would cover that, but until then I\u0027d rather enforce this at build time to guarantee some safety for R-on-Q. IIUC, this is not a problem for native, because at build time you are picking a different variation that was compiled against different stubs, we don\u0027t have these variations for java.",
      "parentUuid": "f5d37e54_9f038031",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "52ab56b583cc86a6771eb8fa6d8afa21b6fbb08b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ae7b5754_a50b967d",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-22T09:35:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think that could guarantee the safety for R-on-Q. The reason is that libB.jar might not be used at runtime. framework.jar is a good counter example. It\u0027s a build-time only artifact. It is never installed on the device (it has installable: false). What\u0027s installed on the device are framework-minus-apex.jar and various framework-\u003cmodule\u003e.jar.\n\nTo guarantee R-on-Q, I think we need the following two checks:\n\n1) everything that are \"part\" of an app (or an apex) should have min_sdk_version: 29.\n2) all the external dependencies (things that are not part of the app or apex) should be satisfied by SDK 29.\n\n(1) is where static dependencies play a role, while (2) is for dynamic dependencies. This cannot be determined just by looking at the dependency from libA to libB. If they are both included in the same app or an apex, we only need to check their min_sdk_version are \u003c\u003d 29.\nIf libA is part of the app (or apex) but libB isn\u0027t, that should trigger an error unless libB is one of the SDK stubs (prebuilt/sdk/29/[public|system|...]/android.jar).",
      "parentUuid": "c39940ac_0f63abf5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "52ab56b583cc86a6771eb8fa6d8afa21b6fbb08b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f1def97f_34063fb6",
        "filename": "java/java.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 159,
      "author": {
        "id": 1061164
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-22T09:51:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As you mentioned, framework is not a dependency that goes into a mainline module though.\n\nRemember that we usually compile java libraries against latest SDKs and have runtime if-checks. You cannot request that all deps compile against SDK 29.\n\nI am not sure I fully agree with your argument there. Note that this covers apps by definition, if an app with msv\u003d29 depends on a list of java_libraries, they all have to satisfy msv\u003d29, instead of doing one check at the root of an app node, and checking every library dozen of times (death by thousand cuts), I am checking at recursively.\n\nAt some point for Java we would have to lift this restriction all together (in S and beyond likely) though.",
      "parentUuid": "ae7b5754_a50b967d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 158,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 159,
        "endChar": 13
      },
